## What I did

Deployed and **measured** the sess87 incarnation landing. The previous session left it built but unverified; this session turned it into evidence.

**Deploy trap found first:** sess87's `make clean` also ran `make -C tools clean`, deleting `tools/mkfs_mxfs`. `make modules` does not rebuild it, and `prep_fs.sh` hard-fails without it — worse, a stale mkfs would have formatted a gen-2 volume that gen-3 code refuses to mount. `make tools`, then prep: 71s against a 240s budget, all 32 nodes on `33018595D555FBE463F017B`.

**Measurements on the live 32-node caw rig:**

- 32/32 live HB records carry **nonzero, distinct** 64-bit incarnations. sess83's identical measurement returned `distinct EPOCH values: [0]`.
- Killed test32 (slot 20, node 2541769084, epoch 4152495875090453247). **All 31 survivors** logged `P163-RECOVERY-PENDING` with that exact pre-kill epoch — detected t+70s against a 134s derived budget.
- `P234-RECOV-FENCED slot=20 victim=2541769084 epoch=4152495875090453247` — the durable recovery descriptor is now incarnation-bound (sess83's item 2).
- Recovery completed: `P163-RECOVERY-COMPLETE`, `P97-SWEEP-DONE rc=0`.
- Rejoin produced a **different** incarnation (16225762881755121090) — it behaves as an incarnation, not a node id.
- Cluster-wide zero `P237-RECOV-INC-MISMATCH` / `-PENDING-REARMED` / `-COMPLETE-REARMED`, no mxfs BUG/WARN (the one `WARNING:` per node is the boot-time `ITS: Vulnerable` CPU notice).

**Instrument fixed en route.** `tests/hb_slots.sh` subtracted a wall clock from `timestamp_ms`, which is `ktime_get_boottime` — it printed **"live ACTIVE: 0" with all 32 nodes beating**. Rewritten as a two-sample O_DIRECT timestamp-advance test; buffered re-reads would have made every live node read as dead. Also added `tests/incarnation_death_probe.sh` as the persistent RULE-3 instrument, which distinguishes epoch=0 (defect) from epoch-nonzero-but-wrong (fabricated identity).

## Criteria: NO

19 open defects, 12 critical. This defect stays **OPEN** — two things are owed and I will not claim them: the **full board at 32/caw** (the regression gate for the proto_gen bump) has not run, and the `P237-RECOV-SUPERSEDED` / `-COMPLETE-SUPERSEDED` / `-SLOT-REOCCUPIED` arms have **never executed on the rig**. My rejoin landed after recovery finished and the slot was already zeroed, so test32 made a fresh claim; those arms only fire on a rejoin raced into the ~124s detect+confirm window. That is unexercised new code on the path that decides whether to guard a live member — exactly where a wrong answer is most expensive. Both are banked in ccmemory and in the ledger's `next`.

